00:03:14.472 --> 00:03:15.044
of the Rings?

00:03:15.044 --> 00:03:21.175
I don't know what your answer to that is.

00:03:21.175 --> 00:03:26.240
So anyway, that was a great conversation
about 21-century mathematics in Norway.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:31.410
So starting some of the things
I want to share with you today.

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:36.110
I am working more closely these days,
really the intersection of mathematics and

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:38.916
learning about mathematics and
neuroscience.

00:03:38.916 --> 00:03:45.108
And we are doing that partly to dispel
some really important myths about math.

00:03:45.108 --> 00:03:46.953
And in dispelling those myths,

00:03:46.953 --> 00:03:51.264
we find it changes people's relationships
with math pretty profoundly.

00:03:51.264 --> 00:03:55.986
So it be no surprise to anyone that I'm
going to start with this idea that we know

00:03:55.986 --> 00:04:00.440
now that nobody is born with a math
brain and nobody is born without one.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:05.290
And in fact, incredible research on
brain Science brain plasticity showing

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:10.340
that everybody's brain can grow and
change to learn any mathematics.

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:13.880
And we need to get rid of the idea that
some students are born with a math

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:15.280
brain and some are not.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.328
So the next slide was animated, but

00:04:17.328 --> 00:04:22.188
it's not going to show up as an animation
in WebEx, unfortunately, but not this one.

00:04:22.188 --> 00:04:27.330
So the brain we know is
our most complex organ.

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:32.083
It's made of over 100 billion neurons, and
several thousand connections between them.

00:04:32.083 --> 00:04:35.970
And how do we grow a map strain,
how did it happen?

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:40.040
This is the slide and they're no
longer animated, but what it would've

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:45.130
shown you is that when you learn something
it's one of three things that happens.

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:48.690
One is a new brain pathway can form.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:52.250
Another thing that can happen is a current
brain pathway that you already have

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:53.950
becomes strengthened.

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:57.730
And then a third possibility is the brain
pathways that you have connect with

00:04:57.730 --> 00:04:59.380
each other.

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:03.220
So when we think about students
having a mathematics brain,

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:08.090
this is how you get one from learning new
ideas that form pathways in the brain.

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:13.190
And then stay with you for you to use,
in your brain, from that point onwards.

00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:16.440
And that seems pretty important for
students to know that that's how brains

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:22.160
form and learn new mathematics and
new mathematics pathways.

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:24.900
And that's really what's
meant by growing your brain.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:28.060
Some of you will know that we've been
sending out messages that mistakes grow

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:31.150
your brain and
mistakes are really good for your brain.

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:33.940
That's what really that sort
of brain growth really means.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:36.850
It's sometimes it's forming pathways.

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:42.094
Some times it's strengthening pathways and
making connections between them.

00:05:42.094 --> 00:05:47.050
So really interesting new research
coming out I think on brian connections.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:53.440
So people wonder what makes people more
intelligent and know more things and

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:58.330
more accomplished and they really try to
figure out what is it about their brains.

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:00.980
Some people think you have to
have to have got a larger brain.

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:01.679
It's not that at all.

00:06:01.679 --> 00:06:05.068
And in fact what makes
people more accomplished,

00:06:05.068 --> 00:06:10.321
more knowledegable is a stronger set
of connections between half the brain.

00:06:10.321 --> 00:06:13.039
As it says here,
the dynamic interplay of neural networks

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:16.595
would operate in concert and draw from
different parts of the brain at once.

00:06:16.595 --> 00:06:21.890
So today, I'm going to talk to you about
how to encourage those brain connections.

00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:24.476
So it's a really interesting
article that recently came out in

00:06:24.476 --> 00:06:27.033
National Geographic And
it was called, What Makes A Genius.

00:06:27.033 --> 00:06:31.191
They were trying to figure out, if we call
certain people in the world, a genius,

00:06:31.191 --> 00:06:33.020
who have just done amazing things.

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:35.458
So they talked about Einstein and
Michelangelo,

00:06:35.458 --> 00:06:37.614
what makes them different in their brains.

00:06:37.614 --> 00:06:41.491
And so they actually have Einstein's
brain in a museum, chopped up, and

00:06:41.491 --> 00:06:43.189
people stare at it all the time.

00:06:43.189 --> 00:06:45.908
There's no difference in size of brain,
but

00:06:45.908 --> 00:06:50.629
what they're doing now is bringing in
people who are super accomplished, and

00:06:50.629 --> 00:06:55.300
having scans of their brains to look
at what's going on inside their brains.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:58.750
And they find, as it says here,
that there's quite a notable

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:01.400
difference between those that
they think of as a genius,

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:06.920
that there is more communication going
on between the different hemispheres.

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:09.056
Those people are more flexible
in their though processes.

00:07:09.056 --> 00:07:12.900
And more contributions are coming
from different parts of the brain.

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:16.090
So clearly this is what we
want to develop in here,

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:20.270
more communication between parts
of the brain, more connection, and

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:22.479
mathematics can play a really
important role in that.

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:26.050
So lots and lots of evidence

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:30.760
that people may be born with differences
in their brains, but those differences

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:36.030
are really affixed by the millions of
opportunities to grow and change brains.

00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:40.029
And all students have the potential to
learn any level of mathematics with

00:07:40.029 --> 00:07:42.138
the right teaching.

00:07:42.138 --> 00:07:46.855
So I want to start to have us
now look into something we

00:07:46.855 --> 00:07:49.330
did at Stanford recently.

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:52.314
That is we brought 81
students onto campus and

00:07:52.314 --> 00:07:56.130
we gave them these different
messages about brain science.

00:07:56.130 --> 00:07:59.400
We told them that mistakes
are really important and

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:00.770
Struggle is really important.

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:03.780
We told them that speed is not important.

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:05.840
And then we taught mathematics
differently, as an open,

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:10.230
creative subject that really backs
up those messages we gave them.

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:13.820
And the new online class that I
have coming out in the summer is

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:16.990
actually based around the videos
that we took at camp.

00:08:16.990 --> 00:08:21.576
Everything was filmed by a filmmaker, and
we're going to watch some of that today.

00:08:21.576 --> 00:08:23.716
This slide's a little mushed together.

00:08:23.716 --> 00:08:27.580
We've lost some of it, so we'll send
this out to you after the webinar.

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:31.560
But we ask, what would happen if
we changed all of these things?

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:34.600
We stop kids thinking that you're
a math person and you're not.

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:37.717
And we replace some emphasis
on the speed and procedures,

00:08:37.717 --> 00:08:39.680
and go more in depth in creativity.

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:44.180
And we got rid of the idea that there's
one way, and instead celebrated

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:50.490
the multiple different ideas students
have and different ways of visualizing.

00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:55.467
And what's lost at the bottom is really
moving away from work being individual to

00:08:55.467 --> 00:08:57.527
being a collaborative process.

00:08:57.527 --> 00:09:04.217
So this graph shows you the pre-scores
of the 81 students we brought to campus.

00:09:04.217 --> 00:09:08.200
It was a test they'd taken in
their district on algebra.

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:13.580
And what you may be able to see there is
81 bars that represent the 81 students.

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:18.750
And you can probably tell there was a wide
range of achievement amongst the students.

00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:21.162
We have very high achieving students,

00:09:21.162 --> 00:09:24.340
we have students who scored
zero on the pre-test.

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:25.129
We put them into four classes.

00:09:25.129 --> 00:09:26.270
They were all mixed.

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:28.750
We didn't have any tracking or grouping.

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:32.705
And then we taught them open,
creative mathematics for 18 lessons.

00:09:32.705 --> 00:09:35.980
They were with us in the mornings for
the math lessons.

00:09:35.980 --> 00:09:41.149
I was a teacher, Cathy and others,
and regular classroom teachers also.

00:09:41.149 --> 00:09:43.150
We had four classes.

00:09:43.150 --> 00:09:47.404
And then in the afternoon they are able
to sort of run around campus with my

00:09:47.404 --> 00:09:48.259
undergrads.

00:09:48.259 --> 00:09:50.470
So they had this day's experience in math.

00:09:50.470 --> 00:09:52.474
And at the end of the 18 lessons,

00:09:52.474 --> 00:09:57.990
we gave them the same district algebra
test that they'd taken in their district.

00:09:57.990 --> 00:10:00.900
And even though we didn't
focus on that test at all,

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:04.920
the scores of the students,
as you can see, had massively went up.

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:08.020
By an average of 50% per student,
which is a huge amount for

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:09.638
an educational intervention.

00:10:09.638 --> 00:10:12.950
Not all students,
as you can see, increased.

00:10:12.950 --> 00:10:16.305
But the increases that you'll
probably notice come from across

00:10:16.305 --> 00:10:17.560
the achievement spectrum.

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:20.320
High achievers,
middle achievers, low achievers.

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:25.232
And that difference is equivalent to
1.6 years of school, so pretty huge.

00:10:25.232 --> 00:10:28.120
These are our kids during one
of their last days of camp,

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:31.790
wearing our YouCubed t-shirts,
making our YouCubed logo.

00:10:31.790 --> 00:10:33.310
Doing a pretty good job.

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:35.420
And I thought we could, well,

00:10:35.420 --> 00:10:40.850
first hear from just one of the students
reflecting on their camp experience.

00:10:40.850 --> 00:10:44.410
They said this.
They taught us how math is for everybody.

00:10:44.410 --> 00:10:46.610
And I believe that I wasn't
a math person before but

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:48.880
now I believe that anybody can do math.

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:49.568
And that helped me a lot.

00:10:49.568 --> 00:10:52.907
And the way I thought the math was all
about the right answers and wrong, but

00:10:52.907 --> 00:10:55.790
it's really about ideas and
it's very creative.

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:58.180
And that helped me like it a lot more.

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:00.530
So I chose this because
it had two things in it.

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:04.270
One was we changed the student's
belief about who they were.

00:11:04.270 --> 00:11:08.549
All 81 of the students on the first
day told the researchers,

00:11:08.549 --> 00:11:10.093
I am not a math person.

00:11:10.093 --> 00:11:13.340
It didn't matter if they were low or
high achieving.

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:17.390
They could name the one person in their
school or their class that they thought of

00:11:17.390 --> 00:11:21.550
as a math person, the one who would
put their hand up really quickly.

00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:22.860
So we changed that idea for them.

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:24.950
We told them there's no such
thing as a math person.

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:27.938
Everybody can do mathematics and
grow brains.

00:11:27.938 --> 00:11:32.250
And then we also, as this student said,
we changed math for them.

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:34.940
So they didn't see it as a subject
as right and wrong answers,

00:11:34.940 --> 00:11:38.210
but one about ideas that's very creative.

00:11:38.210 --> 00:11:41.237
So we're going to watch now a little
film that this filmmaker made.

00:11:41.237 --> 00:11:45.674
And what I like about this film is so
she filmed teaching, but

00:11:45.674 --> 00:11:49.620
she also filmed the students in school.

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:52.745
And she did a little of
what happened before and

00:11:52.745 --> 00:11:55.238
after the camp that we can watch now.

00:11:55.238 --> 00:12:04.856
[MUSIC]

00:12:04.856 --> 00:12:07.024
&gt;&gt; Some people think I'm not good at math.

